Print platform-specific MCP client config and optionally install it.

By default this prints a safe snippet and writes nothing. Use --write to
merge the eshu server entry into the platform config, preserving existing
servers and keys. Use --hosted with --service-url for an HTTP endpoint.

--auth selects the credential story for hosted setup: auto (default)
probes the server's RFC 9728 discovery route and resolves per-user token
or SSO; sso forces the OAuth flow; token forces the per-user
${ESHU_MCP_TOKEN} reference; shared-key (or --shared-key) forces the
legacy admin/dev ${ESHU_API_KEY} credential, never emitted by default.
No posture ever prints a raw secret.

Usage:
  eshu mcp setup [flags]

Flags:
      --api-key string       API key for authentication
      --auth string          MCP auth posture for hosted setup: auto, sso, token, or shared-key (default "auto")
  -h, --help                 help for setup
      --hosted               Generate hosted HTTP setup instead of local stdio
      --platform string      Target MCP client: claude, codex, cursor, generic, vscode (default "generic")
      --profile string       Config profile name
      --service-url string   API service URL (overrides config)
      --shared-key           Force the legacy shared ESHU_API_KEY credential (admin/dev only)
      --target string        Override the file path used by --write
      --verify               Run staged verification (config, reachable, tools, first query)
      --write                Merge the config into the platform's file instead of printing it

Global Flags:
      --database string   Temporarily override database backend
  -V, --visual            Show results as interactive graph visualization
